   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let u be defined by u(x) = k^2 + ln(x) for 0 < x < 1, u(1) = 2k^2 + 3k + 2, and u(x) = kx + 6 for x > 1, where k is a constant. For what value of k is u continuous at x = 1?"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": -2
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The left-hand limit at x = 1 is k^2 + ln(1) = k^2 and the right-hand limit is k + 6.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            [
               "Power",
               "k",
               2
            ],
            [
               "Add",
               "k",
               6
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Equal one-sided limits need k^2 - k - 6 = 0, so k = 3 or k = -2.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Power",
                  "k",
                  2
               ],
               [
                  "Negate",
                  "k"
               ],
               -6
            ],
            0
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The common limit must also equal u(1) = 2k^2 + 3k + 2. At k = 3 the limit is 9 but u(1) = 29, while at k = -2 the limit is 4 and u(1) = 4.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Multiply",
                  2,
                  [
                     "Power",
                     "k",
                     2
                  ]
               ],
               [
                  "Multiply",
                  3,
                  "k"
               ],
               2
            ],
            4
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "Only k = -2 makes u continuous at x = 1.",
         "mathjson": -2
      }
   ],
